.TH COMPXCTL 1 "2026-10-18" "5.0.0-dev" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
compxctl \- inspect and configure CompX USB mice on Linux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B compxctl
[options] command [arguments]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Standalone C17 Linux utility. No Python or ncurses runtime is needed.
With no command, opens the TUI on an interactive terminal, otherwise reads status.
Device writes require an explicit confirmation. Configuration readback does not
prove power-cycle persistence or an effect on report frequency.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B tui
Interactive overview, DPI editor, interval settings, config dump, receiver picker
and capability reference. Requires an ANSI-compatible terminal of at least 80x24.
.TP
.B devices
List supported USB receivers and their USB topology identifiers.
.TP
.B status
Read device state. Individual read failures are reported as warnings and nulls.
.TP
.B battery
Read battery level and charging state.
.TP
.B dpi list
Read all eight DPI slots. An asterisk marks the active level.
.TP
.B dpi set SLOT DPI --confirm
Write an inactive slot 0..5. DPI is 50..6400 in steps of 50.
An unknown active level blocks writes. Do not press the DPI button during writes.
.TP
.B rate
Show descriptor interval and configured host parameter. Frequency is unmeasured.
.TP
.B rate host HZ --confirm
Set global usbhid.mousepoll. Accepts 0, 125, 500, 1000; 0 removes the override.
Requires root. Reconnect affected mice to apply. Rate mapping is for full-speed
USB; the parameter has different encoding on high-speed endpoints.
.TP
.B rate device HZ --persist --confirm
Experimental write of a stored interval code, for 125/500/1000.
Its effect on polling and persistence after power-off are unverified.
.TP
.B probe --start ADDR --length BYTES
Read a window of config memory. Addresses may be hexadecimal; range 0..0xffff.
.TP
.B reset --confirm
Reset the whole USB receiver, affecting all functions including a paired keyboard.
Requires separate USB device access. This does not promise driver restart or
application of the host polling parameter.
.TP
.B capabilities
List supported operations and functions whose protocol is unknown.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --device USB-ID
Select a receiver explicitly, for example 3-2. Required if multiple receivers exist.
.TP
.B --demo
Use a simulated volatile device. No hardware or system settings are written.
.TP
.B --json
Emit schema-3 JSON. Incompatible with tui. Errors include mutation-attempt status.
.TP
.B --timeout-ms N
Reply deadline per attempt, 1..5000 milliseconds, default 250.
This does not bound the kernel's feature-report ioctl or USB reset timeout.
.TP
.B --tries N
Read attempts, 1..8, default 2. Writes are never automatically repeated.
.SH TUI KEYS
1..6 or Tab select a page. Up/Down or j/k select a row. r refreshes. q quits.
In DPI, e edits a slot. In Intervals, h changes the host parameter and s stores
an experimental interval code. In Diagnostics, a chooses the dump address,
p reads 128 bytes, x proposes a receiver reset. Every mutation needs y confirmation.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 success (status may contain partial-read warnings); 1 operation failed;
2 usage error; 130 SIGINT; 143 SIGTERM.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Only PID fa7b is allowed device configuration writes. Sibling PIDs are read-only.
RGB, macros, button remapping, debounce and lift-off distance have no implemented
protocol. No arbitrary memory-write command is exposed.
The protocol has no sequence IDs; a delayed matching reply cannot be proved fresh.
.SH PERMISSIONS
Install udev/70-compx-mouse.rules before systemd's 73-seat-late.rules.
For explicit receiver reset, the separate optional USB rule is needed.
The C implementation requires physical-device acceptance testing before release.
